> Could someone please help me out with this runtime error (below) I am getting. Have tried to run this model (http://cl.ly/131S3n330Z1K, with data http://cl.ly/code/0G3z0o441S0E) on both a Mac and Linux with optimisation on and off. Both give this error I can't interpret. Stan (v-1.1.0) itself is working fine as I can run the example models without error.

This is our bug. You can work around it by 1) restricting xi be slightly positive as in

real<lower=0.000001> xi[3];

and by 2) moving the calculation of the rhos into a generated quantities block at the end instead of the transformed parameters block

generated quantities {
  real<lower=-1, upper=1> rho[3];
  rho[1] <- Sigma_B_raw[1, 2] / sqrt(Sigma_B_raw[1, 1] * Sigma_B_raw[2, 2]);
  rho[2] <- Sigma_B_raw[1, 3] / sqrt(Sigma_B_raw[1, 1] * Sigma_B_raw[3, 3]);
  rho[3] <- Sigma_B_raw[2, 3] / sqrt(Sigma_B_raw[2, 2] * Sigma_B_raw[3, 3]);
}

In addition, although this is largely unrelated to the bug, if you are going to specify a uniform (0,100) prior on xi, then you should specify an upper bound on xi's support, like

real<lower=0.000001, upper=100> xi[3];

in which case, you do not need to explicitly specify the uniform prior on xi in the model block.

With all that, your model seems to run, albeit painfully slowly. There is a lot of new stuff in the Stan manual for v1.1.0 on reparameterizing models like this in Chapter 13.

Ben

Hi--I'm fitting a hierarchical logistic regression with only 798 data points and 8 groups, and it's surprisingly slow!  Perhaps I'm not doing the Matt trick right?  Or maybe I'm not doing vectorization properly?

Below is the code.  I can of course give the data etc. but I thought there might be something obviously bad in the code?

Andrew

---

turk1_code <- '
  data {
    int<lower=0> J; // number of data points
    int<lower=0> N; // number of names
    int<lower=0> y_cat0[N]; // y_cat==0
    int<lower=0> id[N]; // name ids
    int<lower=0,upper=1> is_cat[N]; // indicator for whether the data came from the categorical responses
  }
  parameters {
    real a;
    real b;
    real<lower=0> tau;
    real eta[J];
  }
  model {
    eta ~ normal (0, 1);
    for (n in 1:N)
      y_cat0 ~ bernoulli_logit (a + b*is_cat[n] + tau*eta[id[n]]);
  }
'

Hi,

I am fitting a negative binomial glm as the following:

Y_{k,i} ~ NB(phi, phi / mu_k )

log(mu_k) = X_k \beta

As you see, my data is clustered and I am not modeling any random
effects, yet. This works well.

But when I include an offset to mu_k:

log(mu_k) = offset_k + X_k \beta

Stan works a lot slower compared to the one without offset. I am not
familiar with HMC so I'd appreciated if any one could point out the
reason. And, is there any ways to speed up ?

I put the stan code (for the one with offset) here:

data {
int N;
int k;
int cuts[N];
int counts[N];
int index[N];
vector[k] offset;
}

parameters {
real<lower=0> phi;
vector[k] beta;
}

model {

vector[N] logliks;

phi ~ gamma(2, 0.5);
for(i in 1:k) {
beta[i] ~ normal(0,1);
}

for(i in 1:N) {
logliks[i] <- counts[i] * neg_binomial_log(cuts[i], phi, phi /
(offset[index[i]] * exp(beta[index[i]])));
}
lp__ <- lp__ + sum(logliks);
}

Thanks a lot!

Jianling

On Wednesday, December 26, 2012 11:22:39 AM UTC-5, Andrew Gelman wrote:
> turk1_code <- '
>   data {
>     int<lower=0> J; // number of data points
